#!/usr/bin/env bash
# eo_topic -- open one topic in YOUR discussion file, addressed to another tool.
#
# WHERE: run it at the root of YOUR repository. The topic is written here, in
#        `docs/discussion.md`, and staged. Their tree is read and never written.
#
# **IT CARRIES NO TOPIC, AND ASKS FOR ONE.** What we want from somebody else is
#        the one thing in a topic that cannot be derived from a tree, so the
#        prompt's first act is to ask the person running it and wait. Taking the
#        text in argv was the other design and it is worse: correspondence
#        typed at a shell prompt is one line long, and a topic that says less
#        than a paragraph costs its reader more than it saves us. `--print` is
#        refused for the same reason -- a non-interactive run has nobody to ask.
#
# **WHAT IT DOES CARRY IS EVERYTHING MECHANICAL**, which is the half a person
#        gets wrong: today's date, the commit their tree is at and whether it
#        was dirty when we read it, and **the next free id** -- allocated above
#        the highest ever used *including topics removed from this file*, which
#        lives in Git history and nowhere else, and counting only ids this
#        repository issued. The ids quoted inside a reply belong to whoever
#        opened them, and a run that counted those would issue a number twice.
#
# **ADDRESSING IS NOT CONTACTING.** Writing a topic costs us nothing of theirs;
#        carrying it spends somebody's afternoon, and that is a person's
#        decision every time. Nothing here sends anything, and the prompt says
#        so in as many words.
#
# **A FINDING IS NOT A TOPIC, and the policy's test is mechanical**: if what you
#        want to say has a file and a line number, it is a finding and goes
#        through the reporting workflow instead. The prompt applies that test
#        before it writes, because a finding filed as a topic reaches a
#        maintainer with no reproducer and no state.
#
# **HOW THE WORK IS LEFT IS ONE CONVENTION ACROSS `eo_cmd/`**: staged and not
#        committed, because the diff is the review, and `--push` is the person
#        who ran this saying they do not want one. **Neither of them contacts
#        anybody** -- a push puts the topic on our own remote, where it is still
#        a topic nobody has carried.
#
# THE PRESIDENT IS LOOKED UP, NEVER WRITTEN IN. The register says who holds the
#        office and lives in the office's tree, so finding the file is finding
#        the president -- and the prompt keeps pointing at the standard after
#        the office moves. Duplicated from the other commands on purpose: each
#        is installed as one file and may import nothing from beside it.

set -euo pipefail

AGENT=claude
SHOW=0
PUSH=0
NAMES=()

usage() {
  # --help is a request: stdout, exit 0. A mistake is stderr, 2.
  local out=1 code=0
  if [ "${1:-}" = "error" ]; then out=2 code=2; fi
  cat >&$out <<'USAGE'
eo_topic -- open one topic in your own discussion file, addressed to another
tool of the Eunoia ecosystem.

Run it at the root of your own repository, naming who the topic is for. It
writes docs/discussion.md here and leaves it staged; their tree is read and
never written, and nothing is sent anywhere -- a topic is carried by a person.

usage: eo_topic [--push] [--codex] [--show-prompt] NAME [NAME...]

  NAME            who the topic is addressed to. **Required**, by the name
                  that tool uses for itself
  --push          commit the topic and push it, instead of leaving it staged
  --codex         run codex instead of claude
  --show-prompt   print the prompt and exit, running nothing

**It does not take the topic on the command line: it asks you.** What you want
from somebody else is the one thing that cannot be read off a tree, and a topic
typed at a shell prompt is one line long -- which costs its reader more than it
saves you. --print is refused for the same reason: nobody to ask.

**What it does carry is everything mechanical**: today's date, the commit their
tree is at and whether it was dirty, and the next free id -- allocated above the
highest this repository ever issued, including topics since removed, which is in
Git history and nowhere else.

**A finding is not a topic.** If what you want to say has a file and a line
number, it goes through the reporting workflow instead, and the prompt stops and
tells you so rather than writing it here.

**The topic is left staged and not committed**, the convention every command in
eo_cmd/ that changes a tree keeps: the diff is the review. `--push` commits and
pushes it instead. Neither contacts the tool it is addressed to -- a push puts
it on your own remote, and a person still carries it from there.
USAGE
  exit $code
}

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --push) PUSH=1; shift ;;
    --codex) AGENT=codex; shift ;;
    --claude) AGENT=claude; shift ;;
    --show-prompt) SHOW=1; shift ;;
    --print)
      echo "eo_topic: --print is refused. This asks you what you want to say" >&2
      echo "         and waits for the answer; a non-interactive run has" >&2
      echo "         nobody to ask." >&2
      exit 2 ;;
    -h|--help) usage ;;
    -*) echo "eo_topic: unknown option $1" >&2; usage error ;;
    *) NAMES+=("$1"); shift ;;
  esac
done

[ ${#NAMES[@]} -gt 0 ] || {
  echo "eo_topic: name who the topic is for. A topic addressed to nobody in" >&2
  echo "         particular is addressed to nobody, and the policy refuses" >&2
  echo "         *the ecosystem* and *everyone* by name." >&2
  exit 2; }

if ! US_ROOT=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null); then
  if [ "$SHOW" = 0 ]; then
    echo "eo_topic: not in a git repository -- run this at the root of the" >&2
    echo "         repository the topic comes from. It is written and staged" >&2
    echo "         there, which needs somewhere to stage it." >&2
    exit 2
  fi
  US_ROOT=$(pwd)
fi
US=$(basename "$US_ROOT")

IFS=: read -ra ROOTS <<< "${ANOIEU_REPOS:-}"
SEARCH=()
for r in "${ROOTS[@]}" "$(dirname "$US_ROOT")" "$HOME"; do
  [ -n "$r" ] && [ -d "$r" ] && SEARCH+=("$r")
done

# Their tree, if it is on this machine: the claim goes in front of somebody, so
# it is checked against what they actually have before it is written, and the
# `Opened:` field records the commit it was checked at. A checkout that was
# dirty when we read it is said so -- the file we read is not a file anybody
# else can fetch.
TARGETS=""
for n in "${NAMES[@]}"; do
  found=""
  for r in "${SEARCH[@]}"; do
    [ -d "$r/$n/.git" ] || [ -f "$r/$n/.git" ] || continue
    found=$(cd "$r/$n" && pwd); break
  done
  if [ -n "$found" ]; then
    sha=$(git -C "$found" rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo "?")
    state=""
    [ -n "$(git -C "$found" status --porcelain 2>/dev/null)" ] &&
      state=", with uncommitted changes when you read it"
    wire=""
    [ -f "$found/docs/discussion.md" ] ||
      wire=", and it keeps no docs/discussion.md, so there is no wire to it and
a person carries anything said to it"
    TARGETS="$TARGETS**$n** is checked out at \`$found\`, at \`$sha\`$state$wire. "
  else
    TARGETS="$TARGETS**$n** is not checked out here, so nothing it says can be
checked from this machine: say so in the topic rather than writing a claim about
a tree you could not read. "
  fi
done

PRES=""
PRES_DIR=""
for r in "${SEARCH[@]}"; do
  for d in "$r"/*/; do
    [ -f "$d/scripts/ecosystem/ecosystem.json" ] || continue
    PRES=$(awk '
      /^  "[^"]+": \{/ { n = $0; sub(/^  "/, "", n); sub(/": \{.*/, "", n) }
      /"status"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"president"/ { print n; exit }
    ' "$d/scripts/ecosystem/ecosystem.json")
    [ -n "$PRES" ] && PRES_DIR=$(cd "$d" && pwd)
    break 2
  done
done

if [ -n "$PRES" ] && [ -f "$PRES_DIR/docs/policy.md" ]; then
  STANDARD="Its **president** is **$PRES**, at \`$PRES_DIR\`, and holds the standard:
\`docs/policy.md\`, whose **\`docs/discussion.md\`** section is what binds this — what
a topic is, the five kinds and what each claims, the fields one carries, and who
may address whom."
else
  STANDARD="Its **president** holds the standard — \`docs/policy.md\`, whose
\`docs/discussion.md\` section is what binds this — and there is no checkout of it
here; take it from the link this repository's own README carries."
fi

# The next id, above the highest this repository ever issued. Removed topics are
# in the history and nowhere else, so the history is read; and only `## Dn`, the
# heading of a topic we opened, counts -- `### \`Dn\`` inside a reply is somebody
# else's number being answered, and counting those would issue one of ours twice.
FILE="$US_ROOT/docs/discussion.md"
MAX=0
for n in $( { git -C "$US_ROOT" log -p --all -- docs/discussion.md 2>/dev/null
              cat "$FILE" 2>/dev/null; } |
            grep -oE '^\+?## D[0-9]+' | grep -oE '[0-9]+' ); do
  [ "$n" -gt "$MAX" ] && MAX=$n
done
NEXT="D$((MAX + 1))"
TODAY=$(date +%F)

if [ -f "$FILE" ]; then
  WHERE="This repository keeps \`docs/discussion.md\`: read it for the format its
topics are in and for whether one of them already says this. **The next free id
is \`$NEXT\`** — above the highest **$US** ever issued, counting topics since
removed, which are in Git history and nowhere else."
else
  WHERE="**This repository keeps no \`docs/discussion.md\`**, so opening one is
part of this: it carries the policy's gate banner at the top, in words close
enough to be recognised, before any topic — and it is worth opening only if this
repository intends to read it, because a file with a gate and nobody behind it
advertises a way to reach somebody who is not listening. The first id is
\`$NEXT\`."
fi

# Where the work is left, in the words every command in this directory uses for
# it. Staged is the default because the diff is the review; `--push` is the
# person who ran this saying they do not want one. Neither sends the topic
# anywhere: a push moves it to our own remote, and a person still carries it.
if [ "$PUSH" = 1 ]; then
  HANDOFF="**Commit the work and push it**: \`git add\` what you changed, commit with a
message saying what changed and why, and \`git push\`. If there is no upstream to
push to, or the push is rejected, say so and stop rather than forcing it or
rewriting history."
else
  HANDOFF="**Leave the work staged and not committed**: \`git add\` what you changed and
stop there, so a person reviews a diff rather than a history."
fi

read -r -d '' PROMPT <<PROMPT_END || true
You are opening one topic in **$US**, at $US_ROOT, addressed to ${NAMES[*]} — one
repository of the **Eunoia ecosystem**, a group of tools built around the Eunoia
language and the proofs cvc5 produces, sharing one repository policy and written
mostly by AI agents under light human supervision. $STANDARD Read it, and this
repository's own \`README.md\` and \`docs/\`; this prompt restates none of it.
$TARGETS$WHERE Today is $TODAY.

**Ask the person running this what they want to say, and wait for the answer.**
You carry no topic of your own: if they describe nothing, write nothing. **If
what they describe has a file and a line number it is a finding, not a topic** —
say so and stop, because a finding filed here reaches a maintainer with no
reproducer and no state. Then check what they said against the tree it is about
as it stands on this disk, and **say where you and they disagree rather than
writing the version you prefer** — a topic that is already wrong spends somebody
else's afternoon. Write it at the top, newest first, in the format the policy
gives and this file already uses: the id above, **To:** by the name that tool
uses for itself, **Kind:** — and when it is in doubt it is a \`request\`, because
claiming less standing costs us nothing — **Opened:** with today's date and the
commit you checked their tree at, and **Settles when:**, which is required and
has to be answerable; if what they told you does not give you one, ask again
rather than inventing one. **Never open a topic about somebody else's discussion
file.** $HANDOFF Either way **nothing is sent anywhere** — addressing is
not contacting, and a person decides whether this is ever carried.
PROMPT_END

wrapped() {
  if command -v fmt >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    printf '%s\n' "$PROMPT" | awk -v RS='' '
      NR > 1 { printf "\n" }
      { cmd = "fmt -w 78"; print | cmd; close(cmd) }' \
      | sed 's/\([.!?:;]\)  */\1 /g'
  else
    printf '%s\n' "$PROMPT"
  fi
}

if [ "$SHOW" = 1 ]; then wrapped; exit 0; fi
PROMPT=$(wrapped)

command -v "$AGENT" >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
  echo "eo_topic: $AGENT is not on PATH" >&2; exit 127; }

WHAT="left staged"
[ "$PUSH" = 1 ] && WHAT="committed and pushed"
echo "-- eo_topic: $US ($US_ROOT) -> ${NAMES[*]}, as $NEXT, $WHAT, via $AGENT" >&2
[ -n "$PRES_DIR" ] && echo "--   the standard: $PRES, at $PRES_DIR/docs" >&2
echo "--   it will ask you what you want to say before it writes anything" >&2

cd "$US_ROOT"
if [ "$AGENT" = codex ]; then exec codex "$PROMPT"; else exec claude "$PROMPT"; fi
